{
  "concept": "The logistic sigmoid squashes any real-valued input z into the open interval (0,1), rising smoothly and monotonically through 0.5 at z = 0, which lets a real-valued score be read as a probability.",
  "figureType": "line_plot",
  "elements": [
    "Rectangular plot frame with black border on white background",
    "Horizontal axis labeled 'z' with ticks at -5.0, 0.0, 5.0",
    "Vertical axis labeled 'sig[z]' with ticks at 0.0 and 1.0",
    "Smooth orange S-shaped sigmoid curve, thick stroke, spanning z ∈ [-5, 5]",
    "Formula annotation 1 / (1 + exp[-z]) typeset as a fraction near the top-center of the plot",
    "Light gray dotted horizontal guide from the y-axis at sig[z] = 0.5 to the curve",
    "Light gray dotted vertical guide from z = 0 up to the curve at 0.5",
    "Draggable marker dot on the curve with a coordinate readout label (z, sig[z])",
    "Adjustable horizontal threshold line with value label",
    "Vertical drop line at the z-value where the curve crosses the threshold, labeled z*",
    "Optional shaded decision regions: 'predict 0' left of z*, 'predict 1' right of z*",
    "Faint dotted asymptote lines at sig[z] = 0 and sig[z] = 1"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "dragPoint",
      "type": "drag",
      "teaches": "That every input z maps to exactly one output strictly between 0 and 1 — huge negative z gives almost 0, huge positive z gives almost 1, and the output changes fastest near z = 0."
    },
    {
      "id": "threshold",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Probability threshold",
      "range": [0.05, 0.95, 0.01],
      "default": 0.5,
      "teaches": "That choosing a probability cutoff is equivalent to choosing a cutoff z* on the raw score, and that shifting the threshold slides the decision boundary along z."
    },
    {
      "id": "decisionRegions",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Show decision regions",
      "options": ["off", "on"],
      "default": "off",
      "teaches": "That the sigmoid plus a threshold partitions the whole real line into a 'class 0' and a 'class 1' half, which is what makes it usable for binary classification."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "A curve with two ceilings",
      "narration": "The orange curve never touches 0 or 1 but approaches both. Drag the marker toward either edge and watch the output flatten out — large-magnitude scores barely change the probability.",
      "state": { "threshold": 0.5, "decisionRegions": "off" }
    },
    {
      "title": "The centre point",
      "narration": "At z = 0 the sigmoid passes through exactly 0.5, where the curve is steepest. This is the point of maximum sensitivity: small changes in the score move the probability the most.",
      "state": { "threshold": 0.5, "decisionRegions": "off" }
    },
    {
      "title": "A threshold is a cutoff in z",
      "narration": "The horizontal line marks a probability cutoff; where it meets the curve defines z*. At 0.5 the cutoff sits at z* = 0 — the natural, symmetric decision boundary.",
      "state": { "threshold": 0.5, "decisionRegions": "on" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Raising the bar",
      "narration": "Demand 90% confidence and z* slides far to the right: far fewer inputs are classified as class 1. Lower the threshold and the boundary slides left.",
      "state": { "threshold": 0.9, "decisionRegions": "on" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Lowering the bar",
      "narration": "At a 0.2 threshold almost any mildly positive score is labelled class 1. The curve is unchanged — only where we cut it has moved.",
      "state": { "threshold": 0.2, "decisionRegions": "on" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "The sigmoid does not decide anything — it just compresses an unbounded score into (0,1); the decision comes from where you cut the curve, and a probability threshold is nothing more than a disguised threshold on z.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Single-panel line plot inside a heavy black rectangular frame, white background, generous margins. x-axis spans -5 to 5 with labelled ticks only at -5.0, 0.0, 5.0 (plus small unlabelled minor ticks); y-axis spans 0 to 1 with labelled ticks only at 0.0 and 1.0, label 'sig[z]' rotated vertically, serif/math-style typography throughout. The sigmoid is a thick (~4px) muted terracotta-orange line, smooth, flat near y=0 at the left edge, steepest at the origin, flattening near y=1 at the right edge. Centered near the top of the plot area sits the formula 1/(1+exp[-z]) rendered as a stacked fraction with a horizontal rule. Faint gray dotted guides run horizontally from the y-axis to the curve at y=0.5 and vertically from the x-axis at z=0 up to that intersection. Interactive additions: a filled orange draggable dot on the curve with a small boxed readout showing 'z = …, sig[z] = …'; a dashed slate-blue horizontal threshold line across the plot with its value labelled at the right; a dashed vertical drop from the crossing point to the x-axis labelled z*. When decision regions are on, shade the region left of z* in pale blue ('predict 0') and right of z* in pale orange ('predict 1') with small legend text at the bottom corners."
}

